## Problem Statement

In Rwanda, a large share of the food that farmers grow is lost between harvest and sale. The African Postharvest Losses Information System (APHLIS) estimates that Rwanda loses about 16.3% of its maize production each year, which was roughly 73,000 tonnes in 2020. The same source estimates that the maize lost in 2020 could have met the annual energy needs of about 311,844 people.

Losses happen at several stages of the value chain: harvesting and field drying, threshing and shelling, transport, processing, packaging, storage and sales. Today these losses are mostly measured through national surveys and estimates, and there is no simple database that lets a manager record a batch of crop, follow it through each stage, and see exactly where the loss occurs. Without that, farmers, cooperative leaders and agricultural officers cannot tell which stage or which district needs help.

## Proposed Solution

HarvestGuard is an Oracle database with PL/SQL that records harvest batches and the quantity lost at each stage of the value chain, enforces business rules (for example, a loss can never exceed the quantity in the batch), and shows which stage, crop and district lose the most food.

Scope:The project focuses on stage-by-stage loss analysis using real national survey data.

## Objectives

Main objective:Design and build an Oracle PL/SQL database that tracks post-harvest losses by crop, district and value chain stage, so that high-loss points can be identified and acted on.

Specific objectives:
1. Collect and normalize real post-harvest loss data from the NISR Season Agriculture Survey into 3NF tables.
2. Enforce loss-recording rules with PL/SQL triggers, procedures and functions (for example, rejecting a loss larger than the harvested quantity).
3. Automatically flag batches whose loss percentage is above a set threshold, using a cursor-based procedure.
4. Keep an audit log of every change to loss records to protect data integrity.
5. Present loss trends by district, crop and stage through a live dashboard connected to the Oracle database.

